# Copy to `.env` in this directory. Expo reads it from the app root and inlines
# EXPO_PUBLIC_* values into the JavaScript bundle at build time.
#
# ── `.env`, NOT `.env.local`. ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Expo reads both and `.env.local` wins — but `scripts/stamp-build.mjs`
# OVERWRITES `.env.local` wholesale on every build, to stamp EXPO_PUBLIC_BUILD.
# It writes the file rather than appending, deliberately, so that a previous
# build's stamp cannot linger. Anything else you put there is gone at the next
# build, and the symptom is an app that synced yesterday and reads "Not set up"
# today with nothing changed.
#
# So: your settings go in `.env`. `.env.local` belongs to the build stamp.
#
# ── The server has its own file ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# MONGODB_URI, AUTH_SECRET and everything else the API needs live in the
# repository root's `.env.local`. Nothing from there belongs here.
#
# Nothing secret belongs here. This file's values ship inside the APK, which is
# trivially unpacked (invariant 12). An origin is fine; a token is not.

# Absolute origin of the Fastify API. There is no same-origin server to fall
# back to on a handset, so a relative /sync resolves to nothing.
#
# Without it the app still opens and still records everything — the projection
# it renders is a local SQLite file and needs no server. What it will not do is
# sync: the loop is never started, and the chip reads "Not set up" in the colour
# kept for things waiting cannot fix. That distinction is the point. A device
# that quietly queued all morning, reporting the ordinary offline story, would
# hide the problem until somebody thought to check.
#
# There is no longer any case where it is fatal. A device with nobody signed in
# opens the farm it minted itself (D14) and works — which is what makes a fresh
# clone, where this file does not exist yet, a working app rather than a wall.
#
#   Android emulator  → the host machine is 10.0.2.2, never localhost
#   Physical handset  → your machine's LAN address, e.g. 192.168.1.20
#
# The API listens on 3001 by default.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://10.0.2.2:3001

# ── Google sign-in (A2.4) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OAuth client ids from the same Google Cloud project the server's
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_IDS names. Public by design, which is why they may be inlined
# into the bundle at all — the flow is PKCE and uses no client secret.
#
# With neither set the Google button is not drawn and nothing else changes.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_ANDROID=123-android.apps.googleusercontent.com
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_WEB=456-web.apps.googleusercontent.com
